.TH CBMAP_CONSTRUCT_MP_SCOPED 3 "2026-09-20" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Macros"
.SH NAME
cbmap_construct_mp_scoped \- declare and create an ordered map with a custom allocator, destroyed on scope exit
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cbstmap.h>

cbmap_construct_mp_scoped(hm_name, key_t, val_t, mmgmt_procs);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Declares an ordered map handle named hm_name, together with the companion
type variables that carry key_t and val_t to every later type-inferred
macro, and then creates a map that takes all of its memory from
mmgmt_procs. The macro also attaches the cleanup attribute, which destroys
the map on every exit from the enclosing scope, an early return included.

The macro is cbmap_construct_mp(3) plus that cleanup attribute; nothing else
differs. cbmap_construct_scoped(3) is the same macro with the default
allocator, cbmap_construct_cc_scoped(3) takes a comparison procedure in
place of an allocator, and cbmap_construct_full_scoped(3) takes both.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
hm_name      Name for the map handle to declare
key_t        Key type. It tells every later macro how to read a key, and
             tells the container which order to give the tree
val_t        Value type. Only the type-inferred macros read it; the
             library never gives it to the container
mmgmt_procs  Pointer to a ccol_memmgmt_procs_t, or NULL for the default
             allocator
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
None. This macro declares variables and does not expand to an expression.
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
The macro stops the program through ccol_fatal_err() if the creation fails
The macro needs GCC or Clang, because it uses the cleanup attribute
All four members of ccol_memmgmt_procs_t must not be NULL, and the
procedures must stay callable for the whole life of the map, which here
ends at the closing brace of the enclosing scope
Everything that the map allocates comes from these procedures, and the
destruction at the exit of the scope returns all of it to them
The walk visits the elements in ascending key order, by the order that key_t
selects, as cbmap_begin_iter(3) and cbmap_create_full(3) describe
The exit of the scope destroys the map and clears the handle, so an
explicit cbmap_destroy(3) earlier in the scope stays correct
The companion type variables follow the scope of the handle, so a function
that receives the handle needs cbmap_redeclare(3) there
.fi
.SH WARNINGS
.nf
The handle must not outlive its scope. Do not return it, and do not store
it in a place that outlives the scope: either action gives out a handle
whose map the cleanup attribute frees at the exit of the scope.
.fi
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
ccol_memmgmt_procs_t procs = {
    .malloc = my_malloc, .calloc = my_calloc,
    .realloc = my_realloc, .free = my_free,
};

void tally(int *ids, size_t n) {
    cbmap_construct_mp_scoped(seen, int, int, &procs);
    int one = 1;
    for (size_t i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        cbmap_insert(seen, ids[i], one);
    }
}                                 /* The macro destroys seen here */
.fi
